Both harnesses use the production Gateway HTTP router and authentication, canonical SQLite records, real network requests/responses, and the actual media or cancellation owner. Each revoked request first reaches an observed preparation barrier; the allowed baseline succeeds through the same path.

| Scenario | Observed result |
| --- | --- |
| Authorized bearer / proxy media requests | HTTP 200, exact 68-byte fixture, descriptor closed once |
| Invalid bearer | HTTP 401 before attachment open |
| Credential rotation during real file preparation | HTTP 401; no image bytes or private representation headers; descriptor closed once |
| Proxy allowlist revocation during preparation | Same denial; subsequent admission denied before another file open |
| Authorized HTTP cancellation | HTTP 200 with `killed: true`; production abort invoked once, actual owned child exited after SIGTERM, durable task became `cancelled` |
| Policy revocation after cancellation entered its real lifecycle queue | HTTP 401, no abort/SIGTERM, child remained responsive, task remained `running`; later fixture cleanup used a separate IPC message |

The media and cancellation phases passed in **11.400 s** and **8.561 s**, respectively. The preparation holds only control timing. Cancellation uses an owned synthetic runtime handle connected to a real Node child; its unrelated background `agent.wait` monitor returns the supported pending result. There is no external model or identity provider. Policy publication uses the canonical runtime snapshot owner; the preceding real CLI Gateway proof covers `config.patch` and process continuity.

The sanitized receipt below includes exact source/input hashes, ordered observations, argv with temporary-path substitutions, and cleanup. The complete executed harness sources follow so the composition can be inspected. Raw logs remain private.

